<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <Import Project="../UnityReferenceAssemblies.props" />
  <!--
    A LOCAL TYPE-CHECKER for the package's EDITOR assembly, and the second half of #347.

    Why this exists. `WallstopStudios.UnityHelpers.TypeCheck` covers `Runtime/**` and nothing else,
    so for the whole life of that gate every `Editor/**` file was compiled by CI and by nothing
    local. The gap has the shape TypeCheck's own comment describes - a missing `using` is SEMANTIC,
    so CSharpier parses the file happily - and it has been paid for: a missing
    `using WallstopStudios.UnityHelpers.Core.Helper;` in `Editor/Settings/UnityHelpersSettings.cs`
    produced `error CS0103: The name 'ColorQuantization' does not exist in the current context` on
    two Unity editmode legs with every local gate green, roughly fifty minutes after the push.

    132 of the 139 files under `Editor/` compile here, in the four configurations that ship. It
    compiles the REAL sources against the REAL analyzers, so a WPROTO/WUH diagnostic fires exactly
    as it does in the editor. It is a type-checker, not a test runner.

    #347's actual point is the Odin configuration. Odin is paid, has no NuGet package, and changes
    the base class of public types, so the EDITOR-side Odin surface - nine drawers under
    `Editor/CustomDrawers/Odin/` and the three `WButtonOdin*` files - compiled NOWHERE in
    automation: not in TypeCheck, not in any Unity leg, not in the MCP editor. `-p:WallstopOdin=true`
    is the first thing anywhere that compiles them; `ShimsOdin/OdinEditorShim.cs` declares the Odin
    editor base classes those twelve files bind and nothing else. #275 is that blind spot shipping a
    compile break to consumers.

    ================================ THE REFERENCE ASSEMBLY ================================

    The engine half is `UnityEngine.Modules` 2021.3.33, shared with TypeCheck through
    `../UnityReferenceAssemblies.props`. The EDITOR half is the community `Unity3D.SDK` package,
    referenced for its `lib/UnityEditor.dll` ONLY - its own `UnityEngine.dll` is deliberately not
    referenced, because `UnityEngine.Modules`' `UnityEngine.dll` is a type-forwarding facade that
    already resolves `UnityEditor.dll`'s `UnityEngine 0.0.0.0` reference.

    `Unity3D.SDK` is pinned at **2021.1.14**, and that is TWO MINOR VERSIONS BELOW the package's
    2021.3 support floor. A member Unity added in 2021.2 or 2021.3 therefore reads as ABSENT here
    even though it is present in every editor the package supports and every editor CI runs. The pin
    cannot be moved: 2021.1.14.1 is the newest version `Unity3D.SDK` has ever published, and every
    other community UnityEditor feed on nuget.org is older still - `UnityTechnologies.UnityEditor`
    2020.2.2.1, `UnityEditor` 2019.3.2.1, and `Troikatorz.UnityEditor` at Unity 2018.
    Checked against the nuget.org search and flat-container indexes, 2026-08-28.

    So read a red from this project the way #553 taught us to read one from TypeCheck: a confident
    RED for a Unity nobody ships is the worse failure mode, because nothing in a compiler error says
    which Unity answered. #553 was `[WShowIf]` on a property reported as CS0592 by a 2021.3 engine
    assembly whose `PropertyAttribute` is `AttributeTargets.Field` while every real editor says
    `Property, Field`. The editor assembly is two minor versions further back than that. Before
    "fixing" any error this project reports against a `UnityEditor` member, confirm the member's
    availability floor - and if it is 2021.2+, the file belongs on the exclusion list below rather
    than in a changed source file.

    ============================ WHAT IT DOES NOT COVER ============================

    SEVEN files under `Editor/` are excluded. Every one, with its reason:

      1. Editor/Visuals/EnhancedImageEditor.cs
         Binds `Visuals.UGUI.EnhancedImage` and `UnityEditor.UI.ImageEditor`. uGUI ships as a Unity
         package (com.unity.ugui) with no NuGet equivalent, on both the runtime and editor sides;
         `EnhancedImage.cs` is already excluded from TypeCheck's Runtime set for the same reason.
      2. Editor/CustomEditors/MatchColliderToSpriteEditor.cs
         Binds `MatchColliderToSprite`, likewise excluded from the Runtime set as uGUI-dependent.
      3. Editor/AnimationEventEditor.cs
         `AssetDatabase.SaveAssetIfDirty` (one call site, line ~526). Absent from the 2021.1.14
         reference assembly; present in 2021.3, the package's real support floor, and in every
         editor CI runs. HARNESS GAP, not a package one. STOOD IN FOR, like 5: dropping the file
         also drops the type name, and `Editor/AnimationEventEditorViewModel.cs`, which is NOT
         excluded, carries a `<see cref="AnimationEventEditor"/>` that a consumer's IDE resolves
         perfectly well. `Shims/AnimationEventEditorShim.cs` declares the name and nothing else.
      4. Editor/FitTextureSizeWindow.cs
         `TextureImporter.GetSourceTextureWidthAndHeight` (one call site, line ~656). Same shape as
         3: added after 2021.1, present everywhere the package ships.
      5. Editor/CustomDrawers/Utils/ValidationShared.cs
         Reads `SerializedProperty.managedReferenceValue`. Unity added that property's GETTER after
         2021.1 - the reference assembly carries the setter alone, so the read is CS0154 rather
         than CS1061. Same shape as 3 and 4. This one is STOOD IN FOR rather than merely dropped:
         see `Shims/ValidationSharedShim.cs`, which mirrors its signatures so the eight real files
         that only NAME it still compile - including five of the twelve Odin files this project
         exists to reach.
      6. Editor/CustomDrawers/SerializableDictionaryPropertyDrawer.cs
         Reads `managedReferenceValue` at three sites. Same missing getter as 5.
      7. Editor/CustomDrawers/SerializableSetPropertyDrawer.cs
         CASCADE ONLY. It names `SerializableDictionaryPropertyDrawer` and
         `SerializableCollectionTweenDiagnostics`, both declared in 6. Nothing is wrong with it;
         it cannot compile without the file above.

    Also not covered, deliberately:

      8. `Tests/Editor/**`. That is `WallstopStudios.UnityHelpers.TestCheck`'s scope.
      9. Anything resolved out of Unity's own METADATA rather than a signature - attribute targets,
         parameter defaults, serialization behaviour, drawer registration. See #553 above.
     10. IL2CPP and behaviour. A green build here is necessary and NOT sufficient; the Unity legs
         remain the real proof.

    THREE NoWarn entries are harness artifacts rather than judgments about the sources, and each is
    a specific diagnostic rather than a category:

      * CS0436, exactly one site: `Runtime/Core/Serialization/SerializationFailureException.cs`
        polyfills `DoesNotReturnAttribute` under `UNITY_2021 || UNITY_2022 || UNITY_2023`. This
        project defines `UNITY_2021` (Unity does), so the polyfill activates and collides with the
        copy in Microsoft's `netstandard.dll`. The compiler picks the source type, which is the
        outcome the polyfill wants. TypeCheck never sees this because it defines no UNITY_* symbols.
      * CS1701: `UnityEditor.dll` names `UnityEngine 0.0.0.0` and binds to the facade above.
      * NU1701: `Unity3D.SDK` publishes a bare `lib/` folder with no target framework, so restoring
        it into a netstandard2.1 project is a framework mismatch by construction. Scoped to that one
        PackageReference as well as here.

    Like TypeCheck, this project lives under `Generator~`, which Unity ignores by the trailing-tilde
    convention, and ships to nobody.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>netstandard2.1</TargetFramework>
    <!-- Unity 2021.3, the oldest version in the CI matrix, is the constraint that matters. -->
    <LangVersion>9</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>disable</Nullable>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <EnableDefaultCompileItems>false</EnableDefaultCompileItems>
    <AssemblyName>WallstopStudios.UnityHelpers.EditorCheck</AssemblyName>
    <RepoRoot>$([System.IO.Path]::GetFullPath('$(MSBuildProjectDirectory)/../..'))</RepoRoot>
    <!--
      The EDITOR reference assembly pin, in a property so it cannot drift silently. The engine pin
      lives in ../UnityReferenceAssemblies.props and is shared with TypeCheck and TestCheck; this
      one is ours alone, because they reference no UnityEditor at all. 2021.1.14.1 is the newest
      version Unity3D.SDK has published - see the header for what that costs and why it is fixed.
    -->
    <UnityEditorReferenceAssemblyVersion>2021.1.14.1</UnityEditorReferenceAssemblyVersion>
    <!-- WyRandom uses a pointer for its state mixing. -->
    <AllowUnsafeBlocks>true</AllowUnsafeBlocks>
    <!-- Unassigned-field and unused-local warnings are about fields Unity assigns through the
         inspector and locals kept for readability. They are noise HERE; the real build judges them. -->
    <!--
      XML doc validation, the same switch TypeCheck turned on for `Runtime/**`, pointed at
      `Editor/**`. Roslyn checks doc comments ONLY when it is asked to emit the file, Unity never
      asks, and neither did this project, so a `see cref` naming a member that does not exist, or
      an overload it cannot pick between, compiled clean forever. The package ships Editor as
      SOURCE alongside Runtime, so those comments are what a consumer's IDE reads, and it resolves
      them against the same assembly this project compiles. It costs nothing measurable: the .xml
      lands in bin/ and ships nowhere.

      Two are silenced rather than fixed, exactly as in TypeCheck, because neither is a defect:
      CS1591 is "no doc comment on a public member", which is a different rule from this one, and
      CS1573 is "a parameter has no matching param tag", which fires on every partially documented
      method.
    -->
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CS0414;CS0649;CS0219;CS0618;CS0612;CS1591;CS1573</NoWarn>
    <!-- The three harness artifacts enumerated at the top of this file. Each is one diagnostic with
         one named cause, not a category swept under the rug. -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CS0436;CS1701;NU1701</NoWarn>
    <!-- Same split as TypeCheck: EPS01 is at zero and is escalated, the rest of the family is
         silenced because measured most of it is wrong about Span. Inventory on #517. -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);EPS02;EPS03;EPS04;EPS05;EPS06;EPS07;EPS08;EPS09;EPS10;EPS11;EPS12</NoWarn>
    <WarningsAsErrors>$(WarningsAsErrors);EPS01</WarningsAsErrors>
    <!--
      The four configurations are TypeCheck's four, for TypeCheck's reasons: the WALLSTOP_PROTO
      default matching the shipped asmdef, the legacy define-off fallback, Odin, and SINGLE_THREADED.
      All four branches ship, so all four have to compile.
    -->
    <WallstopProto Condition="'$(WallstopProto)' == ''">true</WallstopProto>
    <DefineConstants Condition="'$(WallstopProto)' == 'true'"
      >$(DefineConstants);WALLSTOP_PROTO</DefineConstants>
    <!-- The configuration #347 is about. See the header. -->
    <WallstopOdin Condition="'$(WallstopOdin)' == ''">false</WallstopOdin>
    <DefineConstants Condition="'$(WallstopOdin)' == 'true'"
      >$(DefineConstants);WALLSTOP_UNITY_HELPERS_ODIN_INSPECTOR</DefineConstants>
    <WallstopSingleThreaded Condition="'$(WallstopSingleThreaded)' == ''"
      >false</WallstopSingleThreaded>
    <DefineConstants Condition="'$(WallstopSingleThreaded)' == 'true'"
      >$(DefineConstants);SINGLE_THREADED</DefineConstants>
    <!--
      UNITY_EDITOR plus the symbol set a real Unity 2021.3 editor defines. These are not decoration:
      `Editor/Tools/UnityMethodAnalyzer/*` branches on bare `UNITY_2021`, and so does the
      DoesNotReturnAttribute polyfill in Runtime/Core/Serialization. Compiling the editor tree
      without them would compile a configuration nobody runs.
    -->
    <UnityVersionDefines>UNITY_2021;UNITY_2021_3;UNITY_2021_3_OR_NEWER;UNITY_2021_2_OR_NEWER;UNITY_2021_1_OR_NEWER;UNITY_2020_3_OR_NEWER;UNITY_2020_2_OR_NEWER;UNITY_2020_1_OR_NEWER;UNITY_2019_4_OR_NEWER;UNITY_2019_1_OR_NEWER;UNITY_2018_1_OR_NEWER;UNITY_5_3_OR_NEWER</UnityVersionDefines>
    <DefineConstants>$(DefineConstants);UNITY_EDITOR;$(UnityVersionDefines)</DefineConstants>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Analyzer only: it ships to nobody and contributes no runtime assets. -->
    <PackageReference Include="ErrorProne.NET.Structs" Version="0.1.2">
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
      <IncludeAssets>analyzers</IncludeAssets>
    </PackageReference>
    <!-- ExcludeAssets="all" because only lib/UnityEditor.dll is wanted; the package's own
         UnityEngine.dll must NOT be referenced alongside the UnityEngine.Modules facade.
         GeneratePathProperty is what makes $(PkgUnity3D_SDK) resolvable below. -->
    <PackageReference
      Include="Unity3D.SDK"
      Version="$(UnityEditorReferenceAssemblyVersion)"
      GeneratePathProperty="true"
      ExcludeAssets="all"
      NoWarn="NU1701"
    />
    <!-- The analyzer Unity actually loads, so a contract the generator refuses fails HERE. It is
         also a source generator the runtime cannot compile without. -->
    <Analyzer Include="$(RepoRoot)/Runtime/Analyzers/WallstopStudios.UnityHelpers.Proto.Generator.dll" />
    <!-- The general-purpose WUH### analyzers ship in the same folder and Unity loads both,
         so both have to be loaded here or a local gate covers less than the editor does. -->
    <Analyzer Include="$(RepoRoot)/Runtime/Analyzers/WallstopStudios.UnityHelpers.Analyzers.dll" />
    <CompilerVisibleProperty Include="WProtoDisableModuleInitializer" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- The editor half. Private=false: nothing is deployed, this project only type-checks. -->
    <Reference Include="UnityEditor">
      <HintPath>$(PkgUnity3D_SDK)/lib/UnityEditor.dll</HintPath>
      <Private>false</Private>
    </Reference>
    <!-- The same vendored binaries the package ships to Unity. -->
    <Reference Include="protobuf-net">
      <HintPath>$(RepoRoot)/Runtime/Protobuf-Net/protobuf-net.dll</HintPath>
    </Reference>
    <Reference Include="protobuf-net.Core">
      <HintPath>$(RepoRoot)/Runtime/Protobuf-Net/protobuf-net.Core.dll</HintPath>
    </Reference>
    <Reference Include="System.Collections.Immutable">
      <HintPath>$(RepoRoot)/Runtime/Protobuf-Net/System.Collections.Immutable.dll</HintPath>
    </Reference>
    <Reference Include="System.Runtime.CompilerServices.Unsafe">
      <HintPath>$(RepoRoot)/Runtime/Protobuf-Net/System.Runtime.CompilerServices.Unsafe.dll</HintPath>
    </Reference>
    <Reference Include="System.Text.Json">
      <HintPath>$(RepoRoot)/Runtime/Binaries/System.Text.Json.dll</HintPath>
    </Reference>
    <Reference Include="System.Text.Encodings.Web">
      <HintPath>$(RepoRoot)/Runtime/Binaries/System.Text.Encodings.Web.dll</HintPath>
    </Reference>
    <Reference Include="System.IO.Pipelines">
      <HintPath>$(RepoRoot)/Runtime/Binaries/System.IO.Pipelines.dll</HintPath>
    </Reference>
    <Reference Include="Microsoft.Bcl.AsyncInterfaces">
      <HintPath>$(RepoRoot)/Runtime/Binaries/Microsoft.Bcl.AsyncInterfaces.dll</HintPath>
    </Reference>
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- TypeCheck's shims are shared rather than copied: the ModuleInitializer polyfill, the uGUI
         ColorBlock, and the runtime-side Odin base classes are the same gaps here. -->
    <Compile
      Include="$(RepoRoot)/Generator~/WallstopStudios.UnityHelpers.TypeCheck/Shims/*.cs"
      Exclude="$(RepoRoot)/Generator~/WallstopStudios.UnityHelpers.TypeCheck/Shims/OdinInspectorShim.cs"
    />
    <Compile
      Include="$(RepoRoot)/Generator~/WallstopStudios.UnityHelpers.TypeCheck/Shims/OdinInspectorShim.cs"
      Condition="'$(WallstopOdin)' == 'true'"
    />
    <!-- The editor-only gaps: UI Toolkit drag events, the Test Runner API, the prefab-stage types
         that moved namespace in 2021.2, and the ValidationShared stand-in. -->
    <Compile Include="Shims/*.cs" />
    <!-- Inert without the define, so it is included only where it is meaningful. -->
    <Compile Include="ShimsOdin/*.cs" Condition="'$(WallstopOdin)' == 'true'" />
    <!-- The runtime is compiled as the editor assembly's dependency, with TypeCheck's exclusions.
         TypeCheck remains the gate that JUDGES it; a divergence here is a bug in one of the two. -->
    <Compile
      Include="$(RepoRoot)/Runtime/**/*.cs"
      Exclude="$(RepoRoot)/Runtime/Integrations/**/*.cs;$(RepoRoot)/Runtime/Analyzers/**/*.cs;$(RepoRoot)/Runtime/Core/Extension/Partials/UI.cs;$(RepoRoot)/Runtime/Utils/MatchColliderToSprite.cs;$(RepoRoot)/Runtime/Visuals/UGUI/EnhancedImage.cs"
    />
    <!--
      The editor asmdef's scope: 132 of the 139 files under `Editor/`. The seven that are dropped
      are removed ONE PER LINE rather than through a semicolon-packed `Exclude`, so each reason
      travels with the exclusion it explains instead of living only in the header, and so the two
      KINDS stay apart. Two are PERMANENT: they bind uGUI, which has no NuGet equivalent at all, so
      nothing short of a Unity install would fix them. THREE are marked PIN-DRIVEN: `Unity3D.SDK` is
      pinned at 2021.1.14, and the member each of those three binds IS present in 2021.3, the
      package's real support floor, and in every editor CI runs, so the FILE is correct and the
      harness is what is behind. Those three go the day a UnityEditor reference assembly newer than
      2021.1.14 exists on nuget.org, and so do the last two, which are the same missing getter one
      level removed: one is stood in for by a shim mirroring its whole surface, the other is a pure
      cascade of the file above it.
    -->
    <Compile Include="$(RepoRoot)/Editor/**/*.cs" />
    <!-- PERMANENT. Binds `Visuals.UGUI.EnhancedImage` and `UnityEditor.UI.ImageEditor`; uGUI
         (com.unity.ugui) ships as a Unity package with no NuGet equivalent on either side. -->
    <Compile Remove="$(RepoRoot)/Editor/Visuals/EnhancedImageEditor.cs" />
    <!-- PERMANENT. Binds `MatchColliderToSprite`, uGUI-dependent and already out of the Runtime set. -->
    <Compile Remove="$(RepoRoot)/Editor/CustomEditors/MatchColliderToSpriteEditor.cs" />
    <!-- PIN-DRIVEN. Missing member: `AssetDatabase.SaveAssetIfDirty`, one call site. Absent from
         Unity3D.SDK 2021.1.14, present in 2021.3 and every editor CI runs. Stood in for by
         `Shims/AnimationEventEditorShim.cs`, which keeps the type NAME resolvable so the
         `<see cref="AnimationEventEditor"/>` in the non-excluded view-model still binds. -->
    <Compile Remove="$(RepoRoot)/Editor/AnimationEventEditor.cs" />
    <!-- PIN-DRIVEN. Missing member: `TextureImporter.GetSourceTextureWidthAndHeight`, one call site.
         Absent from Unity3D.SDK 2021.1.14, present in 2021.3 and every editor CI runs. -->
    <Compile Remove="$(RepoRoot)/Editor/FitTextureSizeWindow.cs" />
    <!-- PIN-DRIVEN. Missing member: the GETTER of `SerializedProperty.managedReferenceValue`, read
         at three sites. 2021.1.14 carries the setter alone, so the read is CS0154 rather than
         CS1061; the getter is present in 2021.3 and every editor CI runs. -->
    <Compile Remove="$(RepoRoot)/Editor/CustomDrawers/SerializableDictionaryPropertyDrawer.cs" />
    <!-- Same missing getter as above, and the exclusion that costs the least: it is STOOD IN FOR by
         `Shims/ValidationSharedShim.cs`, which mirrors its surface so eight real files (five of
         them the Odin surface #347 exists to compile) keep compiling. -->
    <Compile Remove="$(RepoRoot)/Editor/CustomDrawers/Utils/ValidationShared.cs" />
    <!-- CASCADE ONLY. Names `SerializableDictionaryPropertyDrawer` and
         `SerializableCollectionTweenDiagnostics`, both declared in the file removed above. Nothing
         is wrong with this one; it cannot compile without that file. -->
    <Compile Remove="$(RepoRoot)/Editor/CustomDrawers/SerializableSetPropertyDrawer.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <!--
    The engine pin announces itself from ../UnityReferenceAssemblies.props. The EDITOR pin is the
    more dangerous of the two - it is BELOW the support floor rather than merely behind the matrix -
    so it says so on every compile as well, for the same reason: nothing in a compiler error names
    the Unity that answered it.
  -->
  <Target Name="AnnounceEditorReferenceAssemblyPin" BeforeTargets="CoreCompile">
    <Message
      Importance="High"
      Text="[typecheck:editor] Editor reference assembly: Unity3D.SDK $(UnityEditorReferenceAssemblyVersion) (Unity 2021.1.14) - TWO MINOR VERSIONS BELOW the 2021.3 support floor. A UnityEditor member added in 2021.2 or 2021.3 reads as ABSENT here and is a harness gap, not a package one. See this project's header and issue #553."
    />
  </Target>
</Project>
